Q5. Suppose you create an Employee class and override equals() but forget to override hashCode(). What problems can occur when using Employee
as a key in a HashMap or as an element in a HashSet?


1. equals()
equals() is a method defined in Object that determines logical equality between two objects. A properly overridden equals() should compare the fields that define the object's logical identity.

2. hashCode()
hashCode() is a method defined in Object that returns an integer hash value for an object. Hash-based collections such as HashMap and HashSet use this value to determine where an object should be stored.

3. equals() and hashCode() Contract
The fundamental contract is:

If two objects are equal according to equals(), they MUST return the same hashCode().

However, two objects with the same hashCode() do not have to be equal.

4. HashMap
HashMap stores key-value pairs and uses hashCode() to determine the appropriate bucket and equals() to identify the exact matching key within that bucket.

5. HashSet
HashSet stores unique elements and internally uses hashing. It uses hashCode() to locate the relevant bucket and equals() to determine whether an equivalent element already exists.

6. Hash Collision
A hash collision occurs when different objects produce the same hash information used for bucket selection. HashMap and HashSet handle collisions by comparing objects using equals().

7. Logical Equality
Logical equality means two different object instances represent the same logical value according to the fields considered by equals().

8. Reference Equality
Reference equality checks whether two references point to the exact same object in memory. It is tested using ==.

9. Consequence of Overriding equals() Without hashCode()
If equals() is overridden but hashCode() is not, two logically equal objects can inherit different hashCode() values from Object.

This violates the equals()/hashCode() contract.

9. HashSet Problem
HashSet may store two logically equal Employee objects because they can produce different hash codes and therefore be placed into different buckets.

As a result, the Set can contain what the application considers duplicate objects.

10. HashMap Problem
When Employee is used as a HashMap key, a logically equal Employee object may not be able to retrieve the existing value because its hashCode() can point to a different bucket.

For example:

map.put(employee1, "Developer");

map.get(employee2);

Even if employee1.equals(employee2) returns true, map.get(employee2) may return null if employee1 and employee2 have different hash codes.

11. Important Rule
Whenever equals() is overridden, hashCode() must also be overridden consistently.

12. IDE / Java Best Practice
When implementing value-based equality for a class, override equals() and hashCode() together. Modern IDEs can generate both implementations consistently.

13. Mutable Key
A mutable key is an object whose fields used by equals() or hashCode() can change after insertion into a HashMap or HashSet.

Changing those fields after insertion can make the object difficult or impossible to find because its hash-based location effectively no longer matches its current hashCode().


