employee1.equals(employee2)

return true, but I do not override hashCode(), the two objects can still have different hash codes because they inherit Object's hashCode() implementation.

This causes problems with hash-based collections.

For HashSet:

HashSet<Employee> employees = new HashSet<>();

employees.add(employee1);
employees.add(employee2);

Even though employee1.equals(employee2) is true, HashSet may store both objects because their different hash codes can place them into different buckets.

So the HashSet can contain what should logically be a duplicate.

For HashMap:

HashMap<Employee, String> employees = new HashMap<>();

employees.put(employee1, "Developer");

employees.get(employee2);

Even though employee1.equals(employee2) is true, get(employee2) may fail to find the existing entry because employee2 can produce a different hash code and therefore be searched for in a different bucket.

The important point is that hashCode() is used first to locate the bucket, and equals() is then used to identify the exact matching object within that bucket.

Therefore, whenever I override equals(), I should also override hashCode() using the same fields that participate in equality.

Interview summary:

"If I override equals() without overriding hashCode(), I violate the equals/hashCode contract. This can cause HashSet to store logically duplicate objects and HashMap to fail to retrieve a value using a logically equal key. Therefore, equals() and hashCode() should always be overridden consistently."
```




