Q4. Difference between Abstract class and interface. And when would you prefer one over another


1. Abstract Class
An abstract class is a class declared with the abstract keyword that cannot be instantiated directly. It can contain instance fields, constructors, concrete methods, static methods, and abstract methods.

2. Abstract Method
An abstract method is a method declared without an implementation. A concrete subclass must implement it unless the subclass is also abstract.

3. Interface
An interface is a Java reference type that defines a contract that implementing classes agree to fulfill. It is primarily used to define capabilities, abstractions, and loose coupling.

4. Interface Methods
Interface methods can be abstract by default. Modern Java also allows default, static, and private methods with implementations inside interfaces.

5. Interface Fields
Fields declared in an interface are implicitly public, static, and final. Therefore, they represent constants rather than per-object mutable state.

6. Multiple Inheritance
Java does not allow a class to extend multiple classes because this can create ambiguity in class inheritance.

Java does allow a class to implement multiple interfaces.

Example:
class PaymentService implements CardPayment, Refundable {
}

7. Abstract Class Inheritance
A class can extend only one abstract or concrete class.

Example:
class Dog extends Animal {
}

8. Interface Implementation
A class can implement multiple interfaces.

Example:
class PaymentService implements Payment, Refundable {
}

9. Constructor
An abstract class can have constructors, which can be used to initialize inherited state.

An interface does not have constructors because interfaces are not instantiated.

10. Instance State
An abstract class can have instance fields that represent state shared by its subclasses.

An interface cannot have ordinary instance fields. Its fields are public static final constants.

11. Code Reuse
Abstract classes are useful when related classes share common state or implementation.

Interfaces are primarily useful for defining contracts and enabling different, potentially unrelated classes to provide the same capability.

12. Default Method
A default method is an interface method with an implementation, declared using the default keyword. It allows interfaces to provide reusable behavior without requiring every implementing class to implement that method.

13. Loose Coupling
Loose coupling means reducing dependencies between components so that implementations can be changed or replaced with minimal impact on the rest of the system.

Interfaces are commonly used to achieve loose coupling.

14. "Is-A" Relationship
Inheritance represents an "is-a" relationship.

Example:
Car is a Vehicle.

15. Capability / Contract
An interface often represents a capability or contract.

Example:
class Printer implements Printable

The class agrees to provide the behavior defined by Printable.

16. Abstract Class vs Interface — Core Difference

Abstract class:
- Represents a shared base abstraction.
- Can contain instance state.
- Can have constructors.
- Can contain both abstract and concrete methods.
- A class can extend only one class.
- Useful for closely related classes sharing state and implementation.

Interface:
- Represents a contract or capability.
- Cannot contain ordinary instance state.
- Has no constructors.
- Can contain abstract, default, static, and private methods.
- A class can implement multiple interfaces.
- Useful for loose coupling and defining interchangeable behavior.

17. When to Prefer Abstract Class
Prefer an abstract class when:
- Classes are closely related.
- They share common state.
- They share substantial implementation.
- A common base class provides meaningful reusable behavior.
- Constructors are needed to initialize common state.

18. When to Prefer Interface
Prefer an interface when:
- You want to define a contract or capability.
- Multiple unrelated classes should support the same behavior.
- You need multiple inheritance of type.
- You want loose coupling and interchangeable implementations.
- You want clients to depend on an abstraction rather than a concrete implementation.

19. Practical Example — Abstract Class
abstract class Employee {
    protected String name;

    Employee(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    abstract double calculateSalary();

    void displayName() {
        System.out.println(name);
    }
}

20. Practical Example — Interface
interface PaymentProcessor {
    void processPayment(double amount);
}

class CardPaymentProcessor implements PaymentProcessor {
    @Override
    public void processPayment(double amount) {
        // Card payment implementation
    }
}

class UPIPaymentProcessor implements PaymentProcessor {
    @Override
    public void processPayment(double amount) {
        // UPI payment implementation
    }
}

