Abstract class → shared state + shared implementation + closely related classes.

Interface → contract/capability + loose coupling + multiple implementations.

One important modern Java point is that interfaces can also contain default, static, and private methods, so the difference is not simply "abstract class has implementation and interface does not." The stronger design distinction is shared base state/implementation versus contract/capability.

Interview summary:

"I prefer an abstract class when I have a closely related family of classes that share state and implementation. I prefer an interface when I want to define a contract or capability and allow multiple, potentially unrelated implementations. A class can extend only one class but can implement multiple interfaces, which makes interfaces particularly useful for loose coupling and interchangeable implementations."
```


