Q5. Suppose you are designing a payment system where CreditCardPayment, UPIPayment, and NetBankingPayment all need to implement pay().
Would you use an interface, 
an abstract class, or both? Why?


1. Interface
An interface defines a contract that implementing classes must fulfill. It is commonly used to achieve abstraction, loose coupling, and interchangeable implementations.

2. Abstract Class
An abstract class is a class that cannot be instantiated directly and can contain shared state, constructors, concrete methods, and abstract methods.

3. Polymorphism
Polymorphism allows code to work with an abstraction while different concrete implementations provide different behavior.

4. Method Overriding
Method overriding occurs when a subclass or implementing class provides its own implementation of an inherited or interface method.

5. Loose Coupling
Loose coupling means minimizing dependencies between components so implementations can be changed or replaced with minimal impact on clients.

6. Dependency Inversion
Dependency Inversion means high-level modules should depend on abstractions rather than concrete implementations.

7. Strategy Pattern
The Strategy Pattern defines a family of interchangeable algorithms or behaviors behind a common interface, allowing the behavior to be selected or changed independently.

8. Interface vs Abstract Class for Payment Processing
An interface is generally preferred when CreditCardPayment, UPIPayment, and NetBankingPayment all provide the same capability but may have different implementations and do not necessarily need shared state or implementation.

9. When to Use Both
Use both when:
- An interface defines the public payment contract.
- An abstract class provides optional shared state or common implementation for related payment processors.

Example:

interface Payment {
    void pay(double amount);
}

abstract class BasePayment implements Payment {
    protected String transactionId;

    protected BasePayment(String transactionId) {
        this.transactionId = transactionId;
    }

    protected void validateAmount(double amount) {
        if (amount <= 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid amount");
        }
    }
}

class CreditCardPayment extends BasePayment {
    CreditCardPayment(String transactionId) {
        super(transactionId);
    }

    @Override
    public void pay(double amount) {
        validateAmount(amount);
        // Credit card payment logic
    }
}

10. Preferred Design for the Given Problem
For the stated requirement, I would primarily use an interface:

interface Payment {
    void pay(double amount);
}

Then:

CreditCardPayment implements Payment
UPIPayment implements Payment
NetBankingPayment implements Payment

I would introduce an abstract class only if the implementations genuinely need shared state or reusable common behavior.

11. Key Design Principle
Do not introduce an abstract class merely because multiple classes implement the same method. Use an abstract class when there is meaningful shared state or implementation. Use an interface when the primary requirement is a common contract or capability.


