Q . Can a subclass access a private member of its parent class? What about a protected member? Explain what happens across different packages.

PRIVATE ACCESS MODIFIER
- A private member is accessible only within the class in which it is declared.
- A subclass cannot directly access a private field or method of its parent class.
- Private members are inherited as part of the object state, but they are not directly accessible by the subclass.
- A subclass can access the parent's private state indirectly through accessible methods such as public/protected getters, setters, or other methods.

PROTECTED ACCESS MODIFIER
- A protected member is accessible within the same package and also accessible in subclasses outside the package.
- A subclass in another package can access a protected member through inheritance.
- When the subclass is in a different package, protected access is restricted to the inherited context: the subclass accesses the member through the subclass instance/reference, not through an arbitrary parent-class instance.

SAME PACKAGE
- private   → Only declaring class
- default   → Any class in the same package
- protected → Any class in the same package
- public    → Any accessible class

DIFFERENT PACKAGE
- private   → Only declaring class
- default   → Not accessible
- protected → Accessible in a subclass through inheritance
- public    → Accessible

DEFAULT / PACKAGE-PRIVATE
- If no access modifier is specified, the member has package-private (default) access.
- It is accessible only to classes within the same package.
- It is not accessible to subclasses located in a different package merely because they extend the class.

INHERITANCE
- Inheritance allows a subclass to acquire accessible members and behavior from its superclass.
- Inheritance does not override Java's access-control rules.
- A private member of a superclass cannot be directly accessed by the subclass.

ACCESS THROUGH METHODS
- A subclass can access private state indirectly when the parent exposes a public or protected method that operates on that state.
- This is commonly used to preserve encapsulation.

ENCAPSULATION
- Encapsulation means restricting direct access to an object's internal state and controlling access through well-defined methods.
- Private fields combined with public/protected methods are a common implementation of encapsulation.

