Q . Explain method overloading vs method overriding. Can you demonstrate both with Java code? Also explain which one represents runtime polymorphism.


METHOD OVERLOADING
- Method overloading occurs when a class has multiple methods with the same name but different parameter lists.
- The parameter list must differ by number, type, or order of parameters.
- Changing only the return type is NOT sufficient for method overloading.
- Method overloading is resolved at compile time.
- Therefore, method overloading represents compile-time polymorphism, also called static polymorphism.

METHOD OVERRIDING
- Method overriding occurs when a subclass provides its own implementation of an inherited instance method from its superclass.
- The overriding method must have the same method signature as the superclass method.
- The return type must be the same or covariant.
- The overriding method cannot reduce the visibility of the inherited method.
- Static methods are hidden, not overridden.
- Private methods are not overridden because they are not accessible to subclasses.
- Final methods cannot be overridden.
- Method overriding is resolved at runtime.
- Therefore, method overriding represents runtime polymorphism, also called dynamic polymorphism.

METHOD SIGNATURE
- In Java, a method signature consists of the method name and parameter types.
- Return type, access modifier, and parameter names are not part of the method signature.

COMPILE-TIME POLYMORPHISM
- Compile-time polymorphism occurs when the method to execute is determined by the compiler.
- Method overloading is the standard example.

RUNTIME POLYMORPHISM
- Runtime polymorphism occurs when the method implementation to execute is determined at runtime based on the actual object type.
- Method overriding is the standard example.
- Runtime polymorphism is achieved through inheritance and method overriding, typically using a superclass reference referring to a subclass object.

DYNAMIC METHOD DISPATCH
- Dynamic method dispatch is the mechanism by which Java determines at runtime which overridden instance method implementation should execute.
- The actual object's type, rather than the reference type, determines the overridden method that executes.

OVERLOADING vs OVERRIDING
- Overloading → Same method name, different parameters.
- Overriding → Same method signature, subclass provides a new implementation.
- Overloading → Compile-time polymorphism.
- Overriding → Runtime polymorphism.
- Overloading can occur within the same class or across inherited methods.
- Overriding requires inheritance.


👉👉👉👉👉👉👉👉👉👉👉👉👉👉Interview Response

Method overloading and method overriding are both forms of polymorphism, but they work at different stages.

Method overloading occurs when multiple methods have the same name but different parameter lists. The difference can be in the number, type, or order of parameters. The compiler determines which overloaded method to call, so overloading represents compile-time polymorphism.

Example:

class Calculator {

    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    int add(int a, int b, int c) {
        return a + b + c;
    }

    double add(double a, double b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}


Method overriding occurs when a subclass provides a specific implementation of an inherited instance method with the same method signature.

Example:

class Animal {

    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal makes a sound");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {

    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}


Runtime polymorphism can be demonstrated as:

Animal animal = new Dog();
animal.sound();

The reference type is Animal, but the actual object is Dog, so Java executes Dog's sound() method at runtime.

Therefore, method overriding represents runtime polymorphism through dynamic method dispatch, while method overloading represents compile-time polymorphism.

One important distinction is that changing only the return type does not constitute method overloading, and static, private, and final methods have special restrictions regarding overriding.


