Q 3. You have a bank account and two threads simultaneously withdraw money from it.
How can this result in an incorrect balance? Write a Java solution using synchronization.

BANK ACCOUNT
- A bank account is a shared resource whose state, such as account balance, can be accessed and modified by multiple operations or threads.

CONCURRENT WITHDRAWAL
- A concurrent withdrawal occurs when multiple threads attempt to withdraw money from the same account at overlapping times.

SHARED MUTABLE STATE
- The account balance is shared mutable state because multiple threads can read and modify it.

RACE CONDITION
- A race condition occurs when multiple threads access and modify shared mutable state concurrently and the result depends on the timing of their execution.

CHECK-THEN-ACT
- A check-then-act operation first checks a condition and then performs an action based on that condition.
- A withdrawal typically performs:
    1. Check whether balance >= amount.
    2. Subtract amount from balance.
- The entire operation must be protected when multiple threads can withdraw from the same account.

MUTUAL EXCLUSION
- Mutual exclusion ensures that only one thread at a time can execute a critical section that modifies shared state.

CRITICAL SECTION
- A critical section is code that accesses shared mutable state and must be executed safely when multiple threads can access it concurrently.

SYNCHRONIZED METHOD
- A synchronized instance method acquires the intrinsic lock of the current object before executing.
- Only one thread at a time can execute synchronized instance methods protected by the same object monitor.

SYNCHRONIZED BLOCK
- A synchronized block allows synchronization around a specific critical section instead of the entire method.
- It uses the monitor of the specified lock object.

INTRINSIC LOCK / MONITOR
- Every Java object has an intrinsic lock, also called a monitor.
- synchronized uses this monitor to provide mutual exclusion.

ATOMICITY
- Atomicity means an operation appears to execute as one indivisible unit with respect to other threads.
- A withdrawal should be atomic with respect to the balance check and balance update.

DATA CONSISTENCY
- Data consistency means shared data remains correct according to the application's business rules.
- Synchronization helps maintain consistency when multiple threads modify shared state.

