The problem occurs because the bank account balance is shared mutable state.

Suppose the balance is ₹1000 and two threads simultaneously try to withdraw ₹700.

Without synchronization, both threads may perform the following sequence:

Thread 1 → reads balance = ₹1000
Thread 2 → reads balance = ₹1000

Thread 1 → checks 1000 >= 700 → true
Thread 2 → checks 1000 >= 700 → true

Thread 1 → updates balance to ₹300
Thread 2 → updates balance to ₹300

Both withdrawals may appear to succeed even though the account had enough money for only one withdrawal.

This is a race condition caused by the withdrawal operation not being atomic.

A thread-safe implementation is:

class BankAccount {

    private double balance;

    public BankAccount(double balance) {
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public synchronized boolean withdraw(double amount) {

        if (amount <= 0) {
            return false;
        }

        if (balance >= amount) {
            balance -= amount;
            return true;
        }

        return false;
    }

    public synchronized double getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}


Example:

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {

        BankAccount account = new BankAccount(1000);

        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
            System.out.println("Withdrawal 1: " + account.withdraw(700));
        });

        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
            System.out.println("Withdrawal 2: " + account.withdraw(700));
        });

        t1.start();
        t2.start();

        t1.join();
        t2.join();

        System.out.println("Final balance: " + account.getBalance());
    }
}


Because withdraw() is synchronized, only one thread can execute the withdrawal operation at a time for the same BankAccount object.

If Thread 1 successfully withdraws ₹700, the balance becomes ₹300. When Thread 2 obtains the lock, it checks the updated balance of ₹300, sees that ₹300 < ₹700, and the second withdrawal fails.

Therefore, the final balance remains ₹300, and the account cannot incorrectly process both withdrawals.

The key interview point is that synchronization must protect the entire check-and-update operation, not just the balance update, because the balance check and withdrawal must be performed atomically.


